+## XHTTP server: an IPv6 host without a port was answered 404
+
+Commit: `649d3e1c5`
+
+**What happened:** an XHTTP inbound with `host` set to an IPv6 address refused its own clients.
+For such a host on the default port a client sends `Host: [2001:db8::1]`, the address in brackets
+with no port. The server split every Host with a colon as host:port, which this is not, so
+nothing matched and each request got 404.
+
+**Fix:** the server compares the host alone: lower case, without the port and without the
+brackets, on both sides. Test: `TestH_ServerHostMatchesBracketedIPv6`.
+
+## XHTTP: a server with a small sc_max_each_post_bytes refused every upload
+
+Commit: `01053ace8`
+
+**What happened:** the earlier fix for `sc_max_each_post_bytes` of 8192 bytes or less
+(`af9c20a75`, which stopped a crash) raised such a value to 8193. A server that sets the value
+refuses a bigger upload with status 413, so connections to it (`4096-4096`, `1536-6144` in
+subscriptions) connected and then failed at the first full upload. Separately, over HTTP/1.1 an
+upload request that could not be written crashed the app instead of failing the upload.
+
+**Fix:** the client keeps to the value: uploads are cut to it, down to a single byte. A request
+that cannot be written is an error of that upload. Tests:
+`TestH_XHTTPHonoursASmallScMaxEachPostBytes`, `TestH_UploadWriterPiecesFitThePost`,
+`TestH_XHTTPPostPacketWriteErrorIsAnError`.
+
+## Shadowsocks: cipher names that Xray accepts were rejected
+
+Commit: `7b27ba2ee`
+
+**What happened:** Xray accepts several names for the same Shadowsocks cipher, for example
+`chacha20-poly1305` and `aead_chacha20_poly1305` for `chacha20-ietf-poly1305`. Subscriptions written
+for Xray clients use those names, and sing-box refused the outbound:
+`initialize outbound/shadowsocks[...]: unknown method: chacha20-poly1305` (hiddify/hiddify-app#2622).
+
+**Fix:** the Shadowsocks inbound and outbound map the Xray names to the sing-box names before
+creating the cipher (`protocol/shadowsocks/h_method.go`): `aead_aes_128_gcm`, `aead_aes_256_gcm`,
+`chacha20-poly1305`, `aead_chacha20_poly1305`, `xchacha20-poly1305`, `aead_xchacha20_poly1305` and
+`plain`, in any letter case. They are the same ciphers on the wire. Test:
+`TestH_ShadowsocksXrayMethodNames`.
+
+## Tunnel server: a full queue could stop the whole server
+
+Commit: `ed7a5b14a`
+
+**What happened:** the tunnel server keeps up to ten waiting connections for each client. When a
+new one came in and the queue was full, the server took out the oldest to make room, and waited
+for it. If connections through the tunnel had used up the queue in that instant, there was
+nothing to take: the server waited for good, holding its lock. After that no client could
+register a connection and no connection through the server went out, until a restart.
+
+**Fix:** making room never waits: the new connection goes in, or the oldest is taken and it is
+tried again. Test: `TestH_ServerQueueFullWhileDialsDrainIt`, which stopped after about a hundred
+connections before.
+
+## gRPC: a dial to a server that does not answer never ended
+
+Commit: `d9538c993`
+
+**What happened:** the gRPC client dials on its own context and waits until the server answers.
+A server that does not answer held the dial for good, whatever the caller's deadline: the URL test
+of a dead gRPC proxy hung, every later connection of that outbound waited behind it, and gRPC went
+on redialing the dead server in the background, also after the outbound was closed, since Close
+found no connection to close yet. Every test of a dead gRPC proxy left a goroutine and such a
+connection behind.
+
+**Fix:** the dial ends with the caller's context, and closing the client ends a dial under way and
+closes a connection that was made meanwhile. `TestH_GRPCDialEndsWithItsContext` and
+`TestH_GRPCCloseEndsADialUnderWay` hang without the fix.
+
+## Mieru: closing an outbound left its client running
+
+Commit: `0c19e4954`
+
+**What happened:** the mieru client is stopped with `Stop`, and the outbound's Close called a
+generic close that does nothing with it. The multiplexer of every closed outbound stayed, with
+its goroutine and a ticker that wakes it every 5 seconds. Keep top closes each lazy outbound after
+its test, so the mieru proxies of a profile leaked again at every pick.
+
+**Fix:** Close stops the client. `TestH_MieruOutboundCloseStopsClient` counts the multiplexer's
+goroutines before and after.
+
+## XHTTP, simple-obfs: an IPv6 server address crashed the core
+
+Commits: `405fca469`, `e597c0313`
+
+**What happened:** the core crashed (`invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference` in
+`DefaultDialerClient.OpenStream`) as soon as the outbound monitor tested an XHTTP outbound whose
+host was a bare IPv6 address, taken from `host`, the TLS server name or the server address. The
+URL was built as `https://2001:db8::1/path`, which net/http cannot parse; the request error was
+ignored and the nil request was used on the next line. The simple-obfs HTTP client had the same
+bug with a bare IPv6 `obfs-host`, on the first write. The VK and Telemost call transports
+ignored the same error for URLs taken from the services' replies.
+
+**Fix:** XHTTP and simple-obfs put a bare IPv6 host in brackets. XHTTP checks the base URL when
+the outbound is made and returns the request error from `OpenStream`; simple-obfs fails the
+write; the call transports fail the request. A simple-obfs server now also accepts a bracketed
+IPv6 Host without a port. Tests cover an IPv6 host from each source and a URL that cannot be
+parsed; without the fix they crash.
+
+## ECH: a stale ECH config was used for up to a day
+
+Commit: `1ca18bb93`
+
+**What happened:** an outbound with ECH and no static config fetches its ECH config from the HTTPS
+record of its server name (or of `query_server_name`, such as `cloudflare-ech.com`) and kept it for
+the record's TTL. The answer came from the DNS cache, and a DNS rule's `rewrite_ttl` stretched the
+TTL (Hiddify rewrites it to a day). Cloudflare rotates its ECH keys every hour or two, so after a
+rotation every handshake used an old key and failed until the cache expired: a Cloudflare profile
+with ECH connected only now and then (hiddify-app#2539).
+
+**Fix:** the ECH config is still fetched through the DNS resolvers by the DNS rules, but never
+answered from the DNS cache, and it is kept for the record's TTL, at most 10 minutes. A clone of
+the TLS config keeps the fetched config until it expires, so connections do not each send a query.
+
+## XHTTP: cancellation while a response is pending
+
+Commit: `018d3d00563e2addad14cb20e21dd5b54e962ddb`
+
+**What happened:** a canceled OpenStream returned a nil reader while its HTTP goroutine still referenced the named return value. A late HTTP error dereferenced nil and crashed the process.
+
+**Fix:** retain the concrete response reader in the HTTP goroutine. A deterministic canceled-request regression reproduces the panic before the fix; the XHTTP package passes five race runs afterwards.
+
