+Commit: `323b81335`
+
+**What happened:** the MASQUE tunnel waited `reconnect_delay` between failed connects, and nothing
+sets it, so it was 0. With extra security in a WARP MASQUE mode, a selected proxy that failed at
+once, such as a mieru server over UDP whose name could not be resolved, was redialed in a busy
+loop, an error logged per attempt: the app used over 300% CPU for as long as that proxy was
+selected.
+
+**Fix:** without a reconnect delay the tunnel backs off as its client does, from 1 s doubling up to
+1 minute. A configured delay is kept. Mieru's UDP transport also resolves its server through
+sing-box's DNS now (`7a6be6224`), so a server given by name connects.
+
+## A proxy was pinged several times at once, and lowest-delay moved to a slower one
+
+Commit: `635d533ac`
+
+**What happened:** a tap on a proxy, or a group moving its selection to it, started a test while
+the cycle's test of it was running, and a staged test started a second and a third attempt
+beside a slow first one: the proxy was pinged two or three times, and whichever test ended last
+wrote the result, so a late failure replaced a fresh answer and the groups left the member. After
+a start or a hot reload, when every result is cached, the first member to answer took the
+lowest-delay selection at 91 ms over a 17 ms one still being tested, and lost it a moment later;
+each move tested the new pick again, seconds after it was measured, and under load that second
+ping read 2549 ms for a 59 ms member. A member chosen while the fast one did not answer was held
+for the whole cycle, long after the fast one answered again. hiddify-core sent an absolute
+tolerance of 100 ms, which kept a 59 ms member over a 25 ms one.
+
+**Fix:** one URL test per outbound at a time: a test asked for while one runs joins it and takes
+its result. A staged test retries only a failed attempt, at the next mark, and skips the first
+mark for an outbound that worked in this run. Lowest-delay holds during a cycle only until the
+cycle has tested the member in use. A selection measured in the last 15 s is not tested again. The
+`MONITORING` and `LOWEST` log lines say why every test runs and what the group decides.
+hiddify-core sends a tolerance of 20 ms with a ratio of 0.3, and keeps a profile's proxy tags
+stable across its updates, so a hot reload keeps the unchanged proxies and their pings. Then
+(`a85a71b1a`): the first cycle's results arrive one by one and the selection still hopped at each
+faster one, 439 ms to 170 to 112 to 41 to 18 in forty seconds. A member that took the selection
+with a real ping stays for 30 s while it answers, then the best known takes it. A ping cached from
+the previous run decides nothing and is not shown: it only orders the members until the real pings
+arrive, and the app's list sorts by it (`sort_delay`) without showing it.
+
+
+## With a large subscription, the pings changed every few seconds
+
+Commit: `635d533ac`
+
+**What happened:** with a free subscription of a hundred or more configs, the ping the app showed
+changed every few seconds, up and down (29, 44, 33 ms). Three things tested the same proxies
+again and again. A round robin balancer told monitoring of a new selection whenever the members
+it rotates through changed, which is almost every result of a cycle, and each telling tested its
+parents (the Extra Security hop) and its next pick. A member that started working retested
+everything measured through each group it is in, the hop behind the selector too, whether the
+group used it or not. And each regular cycle moved the selection result by result. In the first
+minutes on an unstable network, the home screen said the selected proxy was not responding while
+others worked: lowest-delay stayed on the member it had cached from the last run, since a staged
+test's miss was hidden from the groups behind the last confirmed result, which for that member was
+the cached one, and was even marked as fresh.
+
+**Fix:** only a balancer that sends every connection through one member (lowest-delay) tells
+monitoring of a new selection. A member that starts working retests what goes through a group
+only when the group uses it. During a regular cycle of the schedule, lowest-delay and round robin
+keep the members they use while these answer, and choose with all the results when it ends
+(monitoring tells every group); the first cycle and one after a network change take results as
+they come. A miss of a member with no result of this run but the cached one is seen by the
+groups, so lowest-delay leaves it for a member that answers. The app shows a round robin group by a member that another replaces only when faster
+by more than the lowest-delay tolerance. On the Android emulator with a 160-config subscription
+and Extra Security, the hop's priority tests in three minutes went from 65 to 0, and the ping
+shown stayed the same after the first seconds.
+
+---
+
+## WARP crashed the packet tunnel when it restarted during a network change
+
+Commit: `00f7b6e63`
+
+**What happened:** after a network change, a WARP endpoint with the scanner checks its endpoint
+in the background, and scans for another when it does not answer. When WARP restarted meanwhile
+(a refused device, a change of tunnel), the restart cleared the endpoint's scanner state while
+the check of the start that ended still ran. The scan read that state again to save its scores
+and hit a nil pointer, which ended the whole process: on iOS, the packet tunnel. The first scan
+at connect did the same, its probe reading the state again (h_failover.go:258 in 5.5.1): on
+Windows, right after connecting on a filtered network, when a refused registration restarted
+WARP ("register WARP masque device: context canceled").
+
+**Fix:** a check reads the scanner state of its start once and works on it to the end; the scan,
+the probes, the noise candidates and the scores take it from the check instead of reading the
+endpoint again, and the state is read and written under the endpoint's lock. A test clears the
+state in the middle of a scan, as a restart does; it crashed before the fix.
+
+---
+
+## A DNS rule with bypass_if_failed logged an error for every query it passed on
+
+Commit: `b1ec924c5`
+
+**What happened:** when the server of a rule with `bypass_if_failed` failed, the next rule
+answered the query, but the failure was still logged at error level. hiddify-core's Fake DNS rule
+uses `bypass_if_failed` to pass every query other than A and AAAA (HTTPS, TXT, ...) from the fake IP
+server to the remote rule, so a browser filled the log with "only IP queries are supported by
+fakeip" errors.
+
+**Fix:** a failure that the next rule takes over is logged at debug level. A failure with no
+bypass is still an error. Two tests check both cases.
+
+---
+
+## A masterdns outbound used one of several resolvers on the same IP
+
+Commit: `0e8ed7f69`
+
+**What happened:** with `smart_pool` off, the outbound gives its resolvers to the MasterDnsVPN
+client, whose library entry point kept one resolver per IP. Resolvers on one IP with different
+ports (`127.0.0.1:5301`, `127.0.0.1:5302`, or a server answering on more than one port) became a
+single resolver, so the client's balancer and packet duplication had nothing to spread over.
+
+**Fix:** MasterDnsVPN (the Hiddify fork, `62e94f1`) keeps each IP and port as a resolver of its
+own; the resolver file loader keeps its old behaviour. A test checks one IP with three ports.
+
+---
+
+## The default build left out mKCP, Sudoku, Bond and the call tunnel
+
+Commit: `1c6b0ec6f`
+
+**What happened:** the Makefile's default tags were meant to add `with_sudoku`, `with_call`,
+`with_bond` and `with_kcp`, but they were joined with `/` instead of `,`. Go read the four as one
+unknown tag, so `make` builds and the test runs that use the Makefile tags had none of them, and a
+profile with mKCP failed with "mKCP is not included in this build".
+
+**Fix:** the tags are separated by commas. The full test suite passes with them on.
+
+---
+
+## Links with Xray's merge mask, dialMode or hellochrome_120 were dropped or failed
+
+Commit: `502f16cfa`
+
+**What happened:** patterniha's Xray-core links (and the subscriptions built from them) use three
+things this fork did not know. A finalmask `merge` mask made the whole link fail to load, so the
+link was left out of the profile. `dialMode=oob.0` was ignored. Xray's versioned fingerprint names
+such as `fp=hellochrome_120` failed the outbound with "unknown uTLS fingerprint".
+
+**Fix:** finalmask has the `merge` mask and an `oob` mask, Xray's `oob.` dialMode, which sends the
+first writes with an extra byte as TCP urgent data (ported from patterniha/Xray-core, MPL 2.0).
+uTLS takes every fingerprint name of Xray's lists, in any case, after sing-box's own names.
+
+---
+
+## Monitoring: proxies stayed "waiting" after a hot reload, and stopped being tested when idle
 
 Commit: _pending_
 
-**What happened:** MahsaNG writes the device's key before the `@` and the endpoint, with its
-port, after it, and leaves the endpoint's key out. ray2sing only read a `key` parameter, so the
-device had no key and the config failed with "private_key is not an ECDSA key"; and even with
-the key, a device without `endpoint_public_key` was refused, and a port other than 443 was
-dropped.
-
-**Fix:** ray2sing reads that shape of link (key, endpoint and port, `address`, `mtu`,
-`keepalive`, `transport`, `sni`, `insecure`, `pin`, `ech`, `packets`). The device's
-`endpoint_public_key` defaults to the key of the fixed certificate Cloudflare's MASQUE edge
-presents on every endpoint, the port of an endpoint written as `ip:port` is used, and
-`tls.certificate_public_key_sha256` pins the endpoint by the hash of its key, as the links do.
-A link to WARP is a WARP config: when Cloudflare refuses the link's device (its key was never
-enrolled, or the device was deleted), the outbound registers a device as the profile does,
-saves it as the replacement and runs the tunnel with it.
-
-## DNS and routing: IPv6-first sites failed through a proxy without IPv6
-
-Commit: `bc0447ad8`
-
-**What happened:** Sites that publish AAAA records (Google, YouTube, most big CDNs) were reached
-over IPv6 whenever the device had it, through a proxy whose exit has no IPv6. The connection died
-on the server side, the browser never fell back in time, and the site did not open, while
-IPv4-only sites through the same proxy worked. Routing IPv6 away was no answer: traffic that is
-not routed leaks past the proxy.
-
-**Fix:** Monitoring probes each outbound and endpoint, on its own, for IPv6 reachability and keeps
-the answer in its URL-test history. A DNS rule with `ipv6_by_outbound` hands out no AAAA answers
-while that outbound lacks IPv6, and a route rule with `ipv6_unavailable_via` matches the IPv6
-destinations that would die there so they can be rejected at once. One proxy can carry IPv6 while
-the next one does not; a proxy that gains IPv6 is used for it on the next run. See
-[IPv6 by outbound](../features/dns/ipv6-by-outbound.en.md).
-
-## DNS: a hanging server in a sequential group failed every query
-
-Commit: `900d60c40`
-
-**What happened:** A sequential group walked its servers from the first on every query and gave
-each one the rest of the query's time. A first server whose port the proxy's exit dropped hung
-for the whole DNS timeout, the query failed, and the servers behind it were never asked. With
-hiddify-core's remote group, where the chosen TCP server comes first, configs through such a
-proxy never resolved a name, while the same configs worked with a plain UDP address.
-
-**Fix:** A server that fails is asked after the others for `error_ttl`, still asked when they
-all fail, and forgotten when the network changes. Each server is waited for at most
-`member_timeout`, or, unset, the query's time left shared among the servers still to ask, at
-least 2 s each.
-
-## Monitoring: the selected proxy's ping arrived late
-
-Commit: `f7d6695cb`
-
-**What happened:** The first test cycle took the members fastest-first by last run's result,
-so the proxy the user had selected waited behind every member sorted before it. Moving a
-selector to another member tested only the outbounds routed through the selector, never the
-new selection, so its ping waited for the next cycle. A test asked for (a tap, a selection)
-went through the worker pool, behind a cycle of timeouts that holds every `workers` for up to
-`url_test_timeout` each; its delay was stored only after the IP lookup that follows the URL
-test, which takes as long again; and a proxy that did not answer showed nothing until the
-whole timeout had passed.
-
-**Fix:** Before the first cycle, the outbound each group's selection leads to (a selector's
-pick, a balancer's member, through nested groups) is tested ahead of everything else, and a
-selection change tests the new selection with priority. A test asked for runs at once, beside
-the cycle's tests, in stages (`on_demand_timeouts`, 1 s, 3 s and 10 s unless set): the proxy
-shows as unavailable for now at the first mark with no answer, or as soon as the first attempt
-fails, and the first answer from any attempt replaces it at once, however late. Groups keep
-the last confirmed result through such a miss. The delay is published as soon as the URL test
-answers, the IP information when its lookup is done, and a priority result reaches the groups'
-subscribers past `debounce_window`.
-
-## Monitoring: after a while nothing was tested any more
-
-Commit: `f7d6695cb`
-
-**What happened:** A URL test dials the outbound with a deadline in its context. An outbound
-whose dial ignores that deadline, as a dial into a native library may (cronet's naive dial, by
-the logs), never returned, so its worker waited for good, and so did the cycle waiting for that
-result. A few such tests, and every worker was held: no cycle, no priority test, no ping for
-any outbound, until the core was restarted.
-
-**Fix:** A worker waits for one test at most twice its deadline plus `url_test_timeout` and
-two seconds: the URL test, the IP lookup and one more test after a network reset, each with
-its own deadline. Past that the test counts as failed, its late result is dropped if it ever
-comes, and the worker goes on to the next test.
+**What happened:** Three things kept rows of the proxies page on the waiting animation for good.
+A hot reload (a profile update, a changed setting) replaces the outbounds whose options changed;
+each replaced one started over with no result, and `Reload` could only start a test cycle when
+none was running. In a profile of hundreds of proxies a cycle runs for minutes, so the replaced
+ones waited for the next interval tick. The ticks themselves stop once the core has routed
+nothing for the idle time (three intervals, 30 minutes with Hiddify's defaults), and the app
+watching the list did not count as activity, so with little traffic nothing was tested again at
+all; a tap on a row still tested that one row, which made it look as if only the clicked proxy
+got a ping. And the `Touch` that restarts the stopped workers left the old activity time in
+place, so the next tick stopped them again.
+
+**Fix:** A replaced outbound keeps its last result as a cached one, due for a test, the way a
+result loaded at start is: the groups can fall back on it and the app shows it as waiting. A
+reload then tests the outbounds it created or replaced, and only those, at once and whether or
+not a cycle is running; the others keep their result until their regular time. Restarting the
+workers counts as activity. hiddify-core touches the monitoring while the app's proxies stream is
+open.
+
+---
+
+## IPv6 by outbound: IPv6-first sites still died through a proxy without IPv6
+
+Commit: `d992de248`
+
+**What happened:** The IPv6 probe that 5.4.0 added opened a connection to a public resolver's
+IPv6 address through the outbound and took an open connection as IPv6 carried. A VLESS, Trojan,
+VMess or Shadowsocks dial returns before the proxy's server has connected anywhere, so the probe
+said yes for every such proxy, AAAA answers went out, Chrome, Samsung Internet and the YouTube
+app reached Google over IPv6, the proxy closed the connection and the page showed
+`ERR_CONNECTION_CLOSED`, while Telegram and Instagram, on IPv4, worked (hiddify-app 5.3 and 5.4 on
+Android 16 and 17, #2420). The probe was also traffic of its own to Google and Cloudflare
+addresses, which a filtered exit breaks, and a reset after the proxy's attempt would not have
+helped: an app falls back to IPv4 only when the connect itself fails, and through a TUN the
+connect succeeds locally before the proxy has tried anything.
+
+**Fix:** The probe is gone (`ipv6_probe` is retired). An outbound's IPv6 is verified at its URL
+test, with the user's own test URL fetched once more over the host's IPv6 address
+(`verify_ipv6`), once per run and cached per outbound; a share link may declare it (`ipv6=1`,
+`ipv6=0`, `ipv6_declared`); and a TCP connection to an IPv6 address through an outbound not known
+yet is watched by the router (`route.ipv6_fallback`): when the outbound gives nothing, the same
+outbound is dialed with the sniffed domain, the app's bytes are said again and the connection
+goes on, the app seeing no failure, and the outcome teaches the outbound's IPv6. `ipv6_by_outbound`
+and `ipv6_unavailable_via` are fail-open again: an outbound not known yet is let through, and one
+known to have no IPv6 gets no AAAA answers and has its IPv6 connections refused before the
+handshake. hiddify-core turns IPv6 for the apps off by default and all of this on when the IPv6
+mode turns it on. See [IPv6 by outbound](../features/dns/ipv6-by-outbound.en.md).
+
+## CI: restore compilation and regression coverage
+
+Commit: `a045a8c9678ffff904be367a72136a34b08294b9`
+
+**What happened:** Tests called `Status()` on the upstream outbound interface, GeoIP export still built the old CIDR representation, and libbox contained duplicated report/log functions, missing DNS monitor fields and stale platform references. DNSCrypt certificate queries acquired TCP length framing through wrapped UDP connections. QUIC handshake errors could incorrectly permit fallback after certificate verification failed. Closing WireGuard or AmneziaWG could wait indefinitely for a stalled detour write. Several tests still expected behaviour that earlier fixes had replaced.
+
+**Fix:** Use the outbound status adapter and string CIDRs, restore the libbox monitor state and remove duplicate/stale code. Preserve the packet-connection interface for DNSCrypt. Classify HTTP/3 acquisition and stream errors before allowing fallback. Close tunnel binds before waiting for device locks. Make the AmneziaWG regression wait for its deferred bind before explicitly initiating a handshake, update obsolete assertions, and re-enable the fixed noise layout/count, DNS metadata and round-robin overflow tests. Record missing module requirements and checksums so CI does not resolve new versions on each run.
+
+## Power report: macOS build failure and truncated network counters
+
+Commit: `146b764703defe38a2279caf17c77d648addae8a`
+
+**What happened:** On macOS the network interface reader produced `uint64` byte and packet counts, but the shared structure accepted only `uint32`, stopping compilation. Linux discarded the upper 32 bits of counters, making traffic totals wrap after 4 GiB.
+
+**Fix:** Store 64-bit counters on all platforms, preserve Linux and macOS values, and ignore counter resets when computing interval deltas instead of reporting huge values. Regression tests cover large counters and resets.
+
+
+## Build: v5 outbound status and binary rule-set recovery did not compile
+
+Commit: `bdc860ba937a1c6a983cbb99bed5ef0710a42898`
+
+**What happened:** The fork called `Status()` on every `adapter.Outbound` even though the upstream interface only guarantees `IsReady()`. Recovering CIDR rules from SRS also assigned `[]*Prefixable` to the source JSON fields, which now require `Listable[string]`. An unused import in the inbound adapter broke compilation as well.
+
+**Fix:** Prefer the detailed `Status()` method when an outbound implements it, and fall back to `IsReady()` for standard v5 outbounds. Recover CIDR fields as strings. Remove the unused import and add regression tests for the status fallback and CIDR recovery.
+
 
